🧠 1. Concept
| Aspect             | Functional Programming (FP)          | Object-Oriented Programming (OOP)          |
| ------------------ | ------------------------------------ | ------------------------------------------ |
| **Focus**          | *What to do* — behavior/functions    | *Who is doing it* — objects/entities       |
| **Building Block** | Functions                            | Classes and Objects                        |
| **Goal**           | Avoid side-effects, write pure logic | Model real-world entities and interactions |


🔄 2. State & Mutability
| Aspect           | FP                       | OOP                  |
| ---------------- | ------------------------ | -------------------- |
| **State**        | Immutable (unchangeable) | Mutable (changeable) |
| **Side Effects** | Avoided                  | Allowed              |

Important:
"Immutable" ≠ nothing ever changes.
It means: do NOT mutate the ORIGINAL data — return a NEW value instead.
(See FP_vs_OOP.java: map doubles speeds but original list stays [10,20,30,40])


✨ 2b. Pure Function (FP core idea)
A pure function:
1) Same input → always same output
2) No side effects (no DB write, no System.out, no mutating fields/lists)

Pure:     int square(int x) { return x * x; }
Impure:   int nextId() { return ++counter; }   // depends on / changes external state


💻 3. Code Style Example (in Java)

✅ Functional Style (Using Java Streams):
List<Integer> nums = Arrays.asList(1, 2, 3, 4);
List<Integer> evenSquares = nums.stream()
    .filter(n -> n % 2 == 0)
    .map(n -> n * n)
    .collect(Collectors.toList());
// Declarative: "filter even, then square" — say WHAT, not HOW loop runs


✅ Object-Oriented Style:
class NumberProcessor {
    public List<Integer> getEvenSquares(List<Integer> nums) {
        List<Integer> result = new ArrayList<>();
        for (int n : nums) {
            if (n % 2 == 0) result.add(n * n);
        }
        return result;
    }
}
// Imperative: you write the HOW (loop, if, add)


🧰 4. Java FP toolkit (since Java 8)
- Lambda:              n -> n * 2
- Method reference:    System.out::println
- Stream API:          filter / map / reduce / collect
- Optional:            avoid null checks
- Functional interfaces: Function, Predicate, Consumer, Supplier, BiFunction
- Immutable options:   List.of(), Map.of(), record (Java 16+)


🔀 5. When to use which
Use OOP when:
- Modeling domain (Employee, Order, PaymentService)
- Need encapsulation, inheritance/composition, polymorphism
- Managing long-lived mutable business state carefully

Use FP when:
- Transforming collections (filter, map, group, sort)
- Parallel/stateless data processing
- Writing small reusable pure helpers
- Reducing boilerplate loops

Typical Spring Boot style:
Service/Entity classes (OOP) + stream().filter().map() inside methods (FP)


🧪 6. Concurrency angle
OOP mutable shared state → race conditions → need synchronized / locks
FP immutable data → safer to share across threads (less shared mutation)


❓ Interview Q&A (quick)
Q: Is Java functional or OOP?
A: Primarily OOP; from Java 8 it also supports FP style (multi-paradigm).

Q: Difference between FP and OOP in one line?
A: OOP organizes code around objects and state; FP organizes code around pure functions and immutable data.

Q: Why streams over for-loops?
A: More readable for data pipelines, easier to parallelize (parallelStream), less mutable boilerplate — but not always faster for tiny lists.

Q: Can we use both together?
A: Yes — and we should. OOP for structure, FP for transformations.


One-Line Summary
OOP = objects + mutable state + “who does it”
FP  = functions + immutability + “what to compute”
Java = use both (domain OOP, data flow FP)
